A||N||4||turbo86se||this poor woman!||||1||
Z||000000||turbo86se||12-08-1999||02:18 PM||||<BR>I just got off the phone with my dad in Maryland, recently he interviewed a woman who has a nice red 86 Fiero in her front lawn. She starts it up and drives it around her property twice a week. From what she says, it runs great, and sure does move! Unfortunately she'll never be able to do anything with the Fiero because it's a stolen vehicle. The man she bought it from apparently had stolen it, and sold it to her for $900 cash. She had to have the car, and believed the man when he said he'd bring her the title the next day, well, he never did. Turns out the car has a lein on it, and she doesn't want to pay it off. Now she has a nice red Fiero (I don't know if it is a GT or SE), everything works, new tires, and can't do anything with it. She's most likely going to sell it, without a title. If anyone is interested, including myself, I'll let you know more as soon as I do. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-08-1999||02:28 PM||||I might be interested.  Email me when you find something out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||12-08-1999||03:16 PM||||I believe that is breaking a law if she sells the car knowing that it has a lein on it. The person who sold the car to her is also guilty and she can collect her money from him.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-08-1999||03:49 PM||||Legally, that car belongs to the lien holder. Sounds like trouble unless you can get them to release the lien. I have a buddy that bought a car with a lien on it once. He called up the lien holder and asked how much was owed and they told him the amount. He explained the situation, careful not to tell them who he was or where the car was located. Then he offered them a small fraction of what the lien amount was if they would release it. They decided that a little bit was better than nothing and accepted.||||reg||1
